design:
## Context

A unit given no month-long order spends the month doing nothing, and one unit is easy to miss among
many. This warns about it.

**The bead's premise is wrong, and it changes the work.** It says *"the orders crate has no notion of
order duration today — nothing in `crates/core/src/orders/` distinguishes a month-long order from an
instant one, so the classification is new work"*. It does. `Intent::MonthLong` exists for exactly
this (`intents.rs:57-61`, *"An order that takes the whole month"*), and `Ordered::is_busy`
(`semantics.rs:348-370`) already answers *"is this unit's month already spoken for"* — it is what
stops the teaching check offering a working unit as a spare teacher. So the classification is not new;
it is **one variant short** (`Intent::Teach`, deliberately excluded from `is_busy` because teaching is
itself the thing that check is about) and it is written as a `matches!` rather than an exhaustive
`match`, which is a documented footgun this bead should close rather than copy.

**The real difficulty is elsewhere, and the bead does not mention it.** The app does not model every
Atlantis order. `read_order` (`intents.rs:205-334`) yields an intent for about twenty keywords; the
grammar knows a hundred (`grammar.rs`), and `ASSASSINATE`, `STEAL`, `TRANSPORT`, `DISTRIBUTE`,
`SACRIFICE`, `ATTACK` and others parse fine but produce **no intent at all**. `read_intents` then
drops those lines silently (`intents.rs:170-180` pushes only on `Some`), and `UnitIntents` keeps no
record that a line was there. So today a unit ordered to assassinate is, to any check, indistinguishable
from a unit ordered to do nothing — and a naive version of this check would warn about every one of
them. That is why this bead has to record what it could not read before it can warn about what it
did read.

**Measured, not guessed.** Against the committed turn-71 template — orders written by the navigator
and accepted by the server — the agreed rule warns about **2 of 27 units**, 14451 and 13432, and both
have no orders at all. Both are parked cargo units. The navigator was shown that and chose to warn
about them anyway.

### The verification failed, and this plan is why

Merged, then failed verification on 2026-08-19 (at `origin/main` 1de7528). The navigator: *"Lots of
orders pass for month long, but should not: NAME, ADDRESS, ARMOR, ATTACK, …"* — examples, not a list.

**The defect was not the month-long set. It was the escape hatch below.** This plan said: stay silent
whenever a unit holds any line we could not read. Almost every non-month order in Atlantis yields no
intent — `NAME`, `ADDRESS`, `ARMOR`, `ATTACK` and forty-odd others — so *virtually any unit with any
order at all went silent*, and the forgotten unit this check exists to catch stayed silent with them.
The bead's own purpose, inverted, in the false-negative direction, which is the worse one: a warning
that never fires is invisible.

The fix is to classify at the **keyword** level rather than the intent level, from the rules
(https://atlantis-pbem.com/rules) rather than from what the app happens to model. Two passages settle
almost everything — the enumerated list of month-long orders, and the Sequence of Events, which puts
everything else under *Instant orders*. The navigator settled the two the page leaves open. That
shrinks the unjudgeable set from "most of the game" to "keywords the app does not recognise at all".

Two of this plan's original premises were wrong and are corrected below:

- **`ASSASSINATE` is not a full-month order.** The rules are explicit: *"STEAL and ASSASSINATE are not
  full month orders, and do not interfere with other activities."* It was this plan's worked example
  of an unread month-spender, and it was pinned in a test.
- **`CAST` is not a full-month order.** *"a CAST order is not a full month order; a mage may still
  MOVE, STUDY, or use any other month long order."* `spends_the_month` returned true for it.

Branch from `origin/main`; every line number here is that tree.

## Files to change, and what to reuse

### 1. Record the lines that could not be read — `crates/core/src/orders/intents.rs`

`UnitIntents` (`:116-121`) gains one field:

```rust
pub struct UnitIntents {
    pub unit_id: String,
    /// The `unit NNNN` line that opened the block.
    pub line: usize,
    pub intents: Vec<PlacedIntent>,
    /// Order lines whose keyword this reader does not recognise at all, and `TURN` blocks that
    /// were skipped.
    ///
    /// **Not "every line that yielded no intent"** - that was ah-dwk6's first design and it failed
    /// verification. The app turns about twenty keywords into intents; the rules describe about
    /// sixty, and most of the rest leave the month free (`NAME`, `ADDRESS`, `ARMOR`, `ATTACK` …).
    /// Treating all of them as unknown made a unit holding any order at all unjudgeable, so the
    /// idle-unit check went silent on exactly the units it exists to find.
    ///
    /// So a line is unread only when its keyword is in neither `FREE_ORDERS` nor the month-long
    /// set - which after this bead means a typo, or an order the game has and this list has not.
    /// Carries line numbers so a message could name one; nothing does yet.
    pub unread: Vec<usize>,
}
```

Populated in `read_intents` (`:145-201`), in the `Event::Order` arm at `:161-180`: where `read_order`
returns `None` **and the command is not in `FREE_ORDERS`**, push `line.number` onto `unread`. A
recognised free order is dropped exactly as today — it yields no intent because no check reads it,
and that is not the same as not being understood. **Also** push the line of a `TURN` block opened at
this depth — next month's orders are machinery this reader deliberately skips (`:139-143`). A `FORM`
block is **not** unread: it already yields `Intent::Form` (`:181-198`), and the orders inside it
belong to a unit that does not exist yet.

Every existing construction of `UnitIntents` must gain the field; there is exactly one, at `:158-162`.

**The list itself**, beside `read_order` in `intents.rs`, taken from the rules and from the
navigator's two rulings on the cases the page leaves open:

```rust
/// Order keywords the rules place outside the month, so a unit holding one is understood to be
/// free rather than unjudgeable.
///
/// From https://atlantis-pbem.com/rules: the Sequence of Events groups everything that is not in
/// the enumerated month-long list under *Instant orders*. Two the page never places were settled
/// by the navigator on 2026-08-19: TRANSPORT and its synonym DISTRIBUTE are free; ANNIHILATE is
/// month-long and is given an intent below.
///
/// Keywords that already yield an intent (BUY, SELL, GIVE, TAKE, ENTER, LEAVE, GUARD, CLAIM,
/// WITHDRAW, FORM, CAST) never reach this list at runtime. They are listed anyway so that the
/// classification of every order lives in one readable place.
const FREE_ORDERS: &[&str] = &[
    "ADDRESS", "ARMOR", "ASSASSINATE", "ATTACK", "AUTOTAX", "AVOID", "BEHIND", "BUY", "CAST",
    "CLAIM", "COMBAT", "CONSUME", "DECLARE", "DESCRIBE", "DESTROY", "DISTRIBUTE", "ENTER",
    "EVICT", "EXCHANGE", "FACTION", "FIND", "FORGET", "FORM", "GIVE", "GUARD", "HOLD", "JOIN",
    "LEAVE", "NAME", "NOAID", "NOCROSS", "OPTION", "PASSWORD", "PREPARE", "PROMOTE", "QUIT",
    "RESTART", "REVEAL", "SACRIFICE", "SELL", "SHARE", "SHOW", "SPOILS", "STEAL", "TAKE",
    "TRANSPORT", "WEAPON", "WITHDRAW",
];
```

Matched case-insensitively, as every other keyword comparison here is.

**And two keywords gain intents**, so they are classified by the ordinary path rather than by a
second list. In `read_order`, beside `"PRODUCE"` at `:266`:

```rust
        // The rules' enumerated list omits IDLE, but describes it as "do nothing for the entire
        // month" - so it spends the month, and a unit told to be idle is not a forgotten one.
        "IDLE" if arguments.is_empty() => Some(Intent::MonthLong("IDLE")),
        // The page never places ANNIHILATE; the navigator ruled it month-long (2026-08-19).
        "ANNIHILATE" => Some(Intent::MonthLong("ANNIHILATE")),
```

### 2. Keep the block line and the unread flag per unit — `crates/core/src/orders/semantics.rs`

`OrderedUnits` (`:252-271`) today throws both away — it destructures `UnitIntents { unit_id, intents, .. }`
at `:259-261` and maps to `Vec<PlacedIntent>`. Give it a value type:

```rust
/// One unit's orders as this pass needs them: what it was told to do, where its block starts, and
/// whether anything in it could not be read.
struct UnitOrders {
    /// The `unit NNNN` line of the *first* block for this unit, to hang a finding on.
    block_line: usize,
    intents: Vec<PlacedIntent>,
    /// Whether any line in any of this unit's blocks yielded no intent.
    unread: bool,
}

struct OrderedUnits {
    by_unit: BTreeMap<String, UnitOrders>,
}
```

`read` merges as it does today — a unit written twice has both blocks read as one (`:250-251`) — so
`intents` extends, `unread` is OR-ed, and `block_line` keeps the **first** block's line. `get` becomes
`fn get(&self, unit_id: &str) -> Option<&UnitOrders>`.

`Ordered` (`:280-283`) gains the two, keeping `intents` exactly as it is so no existing caller changes:

```rust
struct Ordered<'a> {
    unit: &'a ReportUnit,
    intents: &'a [PlacedIntent],
    /// `None` when the document has no block for this unit at all.
    block_line: Option<usize>,
    unread: bool,
}
```

`Hex::read` (`:286-297`) builds them from `ordered.get(&unit.unit_id)`, mapping `None` to
`intents: &[]`, `block_line: None`, `unread: false`.

`Ordered::finding` (`:413-430`) takes `at: Option<&PlacedIntent>` and reads `line`/`column_start`/
`column_end` from it. **It is not changed.** This check needs a finding on a *line* with no columns,
which it cannot express — so add one method beside it:

```rust
    /// A finding against the unit's block rather than against one order in it, for a check whose
    /// subject is the absence of an order. No columns: there is no token to underline.
    fn finding_at_block(&self, hex: &Hex<'_>, code: Code, message: String) -> Finding {
        Finding {
            code,
            message,
            region_id: hex.region.region_id.clone(),
            unit_id: Some(self.unit.unit_id.clone()),
            line: self.block_line,
            column_start: None,
            column_end: None,
        }
    }
```

`line: self.block_line` is already `Option<usize>`, so a unit with **no block at all** yields a
finding with no line — which the navigator chose (see *User-facing decisions*, Q5). `RegionPanel.tsx`'s
`Problems` (`:243`) and `ProblemsPanel.tsx` (`:17`) render the message without a line and need no
change; `OrdersPanel.tsx`'s `ProblemList` (`:190`) does print `line {problem.lineStart}`, but it is
handed only the problems of the block being edited (`:171`, `validatedBlock`), and a unit with no
block has no block being edited. **Verify that by hand** — it is the one claim here that rests on a
prop rather than on a type.

### 3. What spends a month — `crates/core/src/orders/semantics.rs`

```rust
/// Whether this order consumes the unit's month.
///
/// Written as an exhaustive `match` on purpose. `is_busy` below was a `matches!` with a fixed list,
/// and a variant missing from such a list returns `false` silently - the failure `ah-90w` had to
/// write a regression test against when `BUILD` gained a shape. Here the compiler refuses a new
/// `Intent` variant until somebody has said which side of this line it falls on.
fn spends_the_month(intent: &Intent) -> bool {
    match intent {
        // The rules' enumerated list, plus IDLE and ANNIHILATE, which reach here as `MonthLong`.
        // ADVANCE arrives as `Move` - `is_movement_command` covers it (movement/orders.rs:48-52).
        Intent::Study { .. }
        | Intent::Teach { .. }
        | Intent::Tax
        | Intent::Pillage
        | Intent::Work
        | Intent::Entertain
        | Intent::Move { .. }
        | Intent::Sail { .. }
        | Intent::Build { .. } => true,

        // CAST is NOT a full month order: "a mage may still MOVE, STUDY, or use any other month
        // long order". A bare CAST falls back to `MonthLong("CAST")` (intents.rs:329), so it has
        // to be caught before the arm below - which is why these two arms are in this order.
        Intent::MonthLong("CAST") => false,
        Intent::MonthLong(_) => true,

        // Each of these leaves the month free. GUARD is a flag rather than a month's work - a
        // guard can tax as well - and FORM only asks for a unit to exist.
        Intent::Cast { .. }
        | Intent::Give { .. }
        | Intent::Take { .. }
        | Intent::Buy { .. }
        | Intent::Sell { .. }
        | Intent::Guard(_)
        | Intent::Claim(_)
        | Intent::Withdraw
        | Intent::Form { .. }
        | Intent::Enter { .. }
        | Intent::Leave => false,
    }
}
```

That is all twenty-one variants of `Intent` (`intents.rs:19-104`); no `_` arm, deliberately.
`Intent::MonthLong(_)` now covers `PRODUCE` (`intents.rs:266`), `IDLE`, `ANNIHILATE`, and the
bare-`CAST` fallback that the arm above excludes.

**`is_busy` keeps CAST, and that is a deliberate exception rather than an oversight.** The obvious
rewrite — `spends_the_month(intent) && !matches!(intent, Intent::Teach { .. })` — would change a
shipped warning: every mage that only casts would become a candidate spare teacher and collect
`teacher-has-free-slots` findings it does not get today. The navigator was asked and chose to keep
this bead out of that. So:

```rust
    fn is_busy(&self) -> bool {
        self.intents().any(|intent| match intent {
            // Teaching is what the spare-teacher check is about, so it never counts here. This is
            // the exclusion the doc comment below has always described.
            Intent::Teach { .. } => false,
            // CAST leaves the month free by the rules, and `spends_the_month` says so - but this
            // check has always counted it, and following the rules here would add spare-teacher
            // findings on every casting mage. ah-dwk6 was not asked to change that warning.
            // `ah-vw63` narrows this check to units that actually TEACH, and when it lands this
            // arm should go and CAST should be classified by the rules in both places.
            Intent::Cast { .. } | Intent::MonthLong("CAST") => true,
            other => spends_the_month(other),
        })
    }
```

That still removes the footgun its doc comment describes — a new `Intent` variant is now classified
once, in `spends_the_month`'s exhaustive match, and reaches `is_busy` through the `other` arm rather
than through a `matches!` list that can silently fall behind.

### 4. The check — `check_idle_units`

A new code in `mod codes` (`:73-124`), and appended **last** to `ALL`, whose length goes from
`[Code; 20]` (`:104`) to `[Code; 21]`:

```rust
    pub const UNIT_DOES_NOTHING: Code = Code("unit-does-nothing");
```

The function, called from `check_turn`'s per-hex list (`:221-232`) after `check_forms`:

```rust
/// Every own unit whose orders, all of which we could read, spend none of its month.
fn check_idle_units(hex: &Hex<'_>, options: &CheckOptions, findings: &mut Vec<Finding>) {
    if !options.emits(codes::UNIT_DOES_NOTHING) {
        return;
    }

    for ordered in &hex.units {
        // A line we could not read may well be a month's work - `ASSASSINATE 4021` is - so a unit
        // holding one is not judged at all. Silence here is the price of never being wrong.
        if ordered.unread {
            continue;
        }
        if ordered.intents().any(spends_the_month) {
            continue;
        }
        findings.push(ordered.finding_at_block(
            hex,
            codes::UNIT_DOES_NOTHING,
            "has no order that spends the month".to_string(),
        ));
    }
}
```

`hex.units` is own units only (`Hex::read` filters `unit.own`, `:290`), so foreign units are excluded
for free. `ordered.intents()` is the existing iterator at `:319`.

**No exemptions.** Not for an empty block, not for `GUARD`, not for a unit in the ocean — the
navigator chose the plain rule over all three (Q1, Q2). A unit with no orders at all reaches the loop
with `intents: &[]` and `unread: false`, so it warns, which is the case the bead exists for.

### 5. The settings tab — `packages/shared/src/workspace/SettingsDialog.tsx`

Appended to the existing **Orders** group (`:411-439`), after `unit-overloaded` (`:433-437`):

```tsx
      {
        code: "unit-does-nothing",
        title: "Units that do nothing",
        description: "A unit with no order that spends its month."
      }
```

Both strings are the navigator's. **On by default** — `DEFAULT_ADVISORY_CHECKS`
(`packages/shared/src/settingsStore.ts:26-35`) derives defaults as `code !== "hex-unguarded"`, so this
needs no edit and no exception.

### 6. The generated vocabulary

```bash
ATLANTIS_UPDATE_GENERATED=1 cargo test -p atlantis-hud-core --test generated_ts
```

## Increments

1. **The reader tells a free order from an unknown one.**
   RED: `intents.rs` tests — `a_free_order_is_not_unread` (`unit 5\nNAME "Scouts"\n` → `intents`
   empty, `unread` **empty**: this is the verification failure, written as a test),
   `an_unknown_keyword_is_recorded_as_unread` (`unit 5\nFLIBBERTIGIBBET\n` → `unread == vec![2]`),
   `assassinating_is_free` and `stealing_is_free` (the rules' own example pair, and this plan's
   original counter-example), `a_turn_block_is_recorded_as_unread`, and `a_form_block_is_not_unread`.
   They do not compile.
   GREEN: the `unread` field, `FREE_ORDERS`, and the guarded push in `read_intents`.

2. **The month, classified from the rules and exhaustively.**
   RED: `semantics.rs` — `teaching_spends_the_month`, `giving_does_not_spend_the_month`,
   **`casting_does_not_spend_the_month`** and **`a_bare_cast_does_not_spend_the_month`** (the
   `MonthLong("CAST")` fallback, which is why the two `MonthLong` arms are ordered as they are),
   `idling_spends_the_month`, `annihilating_spends_the_month`, and
   `advancing_spends_the_month` (`ADVANCE` arrives as `Intent::Move`). All call `spends_the_month`
   directly.
   GREEN: `spends_the_month`, the two new `read_order` arms, and `is_busy` rewritten with its CAST
   exception.
   **Also RED first, and this is the point of the increment:**
   `a_unit_teaching_is_still_not_offered_as_a_spare_teacher` and
   **`a_casting_mage_is_still_not_offered_as_a_spare_teacher`** — both assert the existing
   `teacher-has-free-slots` behaviour is *unchanged*. Both pass today; both must still pass. The
   second is the guard on the deliberate CAST exception, and if it fails, the exception was dropped
   and a shipped warning has changed.

3. **The block line and the unread flag reach the checks.**
   RED: nothing observable yet — this is the plumbing. Write it as a compile-driven step: change
   `OrderedUnits`, `UnitOrders`, `Ordered` and `Hex::read`, and let the compiler find every caller.
   GREEN: `cargo test --workspace` is green again with no behaviour change. **No new test here**;
   increment 4 is what exercises it.

4. **A unit with nothing to do is warned about.**
   RED: `a_unit_with_no_orders_at_all_is_warned` — one own unit, orders `"unit 4021\n"`, expecting one
   finding, code `unit-does-nothing`, `unit_id` `Some("4021")`, `line` `Some(1)`, message
   `has no order that spends the month`; and `a_unit_that_only_gives_is_warned`
   (`unit 4021\nGIVE 4022 10 SILV\n` → warned, `line` `Some(1)` — the block line, not the GIVE line).
   GREEN: `codes::UNIT_DOES_NOTHING`, `ALL` at `[Code; 21]`, `finding_at_block`, `check_idle_units`,
   and its call.

5. **Every silence it owes.**
   RED: `a_unit_that_works_is_silent`, `a_unit_that_teaches_is_silent`, `a_unit_that_guards_is_warned`
   (GUARD is not work — the navigator's choice, pinned so nobody "fixes" it),
   **`a_unit_whose_only_order_is_free_is_warned`** (`NAME "Scouts"` — the verification failure at
   the check's own level), `a_unit_that_only_assassinates_is_warned` (the rules: not a full month
   order), `a_unit_that_only_casts_is_warned`,
   `a_unit_with_an_unknown_keyword_is_silent` (`FLIBBERTIGIBBET`),
   `a_unit_that_is_idle_is_silent` (`IDLE` — the player said so),
   `a_unit_with_a_turn_block_is_silent`, `a_foreign_unit_doing_nothing_is_not_warned_about`,
   `a_unit_with_no_block_in_the_document_is_warned_with_no_line` (`line` is `None`), and
   `the_idle_check_can_be_turned_off` via `disabling(code)` (`:4775`), plus its row in the disable
   table.
   GREEN: the `unread` and `spends_the_month` branches.

6. **The real turn admits two more findings.**
   RED: `crates/core/tests/validate_real_orders.rs` fails in three places (see *The test plan*).
   GREEN: update all three, having **checked** that each added entry is `unit-does-nothing` against
   unit 14451 and unit 13432 and nothing else.

7. **The vocabulary and the tab.**
   RED: `generated_ts.rs:52` fails; `SettingsDialog.test.tsx`'s hard-coded `titles` array fails.
   GREEN: regenerate; add the `WARNING_GROUPS` entry; add "Units that do nothing" to the `titles`
   array (`SettingsDialog.test.tsx:67-85`) in the position the Orders group renders it.

## The test plan

