## Known traps

- **The bead's own description is wrong about the starting point.** It says order duration does not
  exist; `Intent::MonthLong` and `is_busy` are exactly that. Do not build a second classification
  beside them — build the one and rewrite `is_busy` on top of it.
- **`is_busy` is a `matches!` and deliberately omits `Intent::Teach`.** Its doc comment says why. The
  rewrite must preserve that exclusion exactly, or the teaching check starts offering teachers as
  their own spare teachers. `a_unit_teaching_is_still_not_offered_as_a_spare_teacher` is the guard.
- **The unread rule is the whole danger of this bead, and getting it too WIDE is what failed the
  verification.** `read_order` returns `None` for about forty keywords, and the rules say almost all
  of them leave the month free. Treating "no intent" as "cannot judge" silences the check for any
  unit holding a single `NAME` or `ADDRESS` line — which is most units, and precisely the forgotten
  ones. The escape hatch must be keyed on `FREE_ORDERS`, not on `None`.
- **The two `MonthLong` arms are order-dependent.** `Intent::MonthLong("CAST")` must be matched
  before `Intent::MonthLong(_)`, or the bare-`CAST` fallback (`intents.rs:329`) is classified
  month-long and the CAST correction silently does not apply to it.
- **`is_busy`'s CAST arm is deliberate and is not dead code.** Removing it "for consistency" with
  `spends_the_month` changes a shipped warning — every casting mage becomes a candidate spare
  teacher. `a_casting_mage_is_still_not_offered_as_a_spare_teacher` is the guard, and `ah-vw63` is
  where it is meant to be removed.
- **`ADVANCE` is not a keyword in `read_order`.** It arrives as `Intent::Move` via
  `is_movement_command` (`movement/orders.rs:48-52`), so it needs no entry in either list. Adding one
  is harmless but adding it to `FREE_ORDERS` would be wrong.
- **`codes::ALL` has its length in its type** (`:104`) and the generated TypeScript copies its order:
  append last, bump to `[Code; 21]`, regenerate.
- **`validate_real_orders.rs` will fail, and that is correct.** Three assertions and their comments
  say the turn carries eight findings. Change them to ten only after confirming the two added ones
  are `unit-does-nothing` against 14451 and 13432. If a third appears, something is wrong with the
  check, not with the fixture.
- **A unit written twice has both blocks read as one** (`semantics.rs:250-251`). `unread` must be
  OR-ed across them and `block_line` must keep the first, or a unit with a clean first block and an
  unreadable second one gets judged on half its orders.
- **`Ordered::finding` cannot express this finding.** It takes columns from a `PlacedIntent`, and
  there is no offending token here — the subject is an order that is absent. Use `finding_at_block`;
  do not invent a `PlacedIntent` to point at.
- **`OrdersPanel.tsx` prints `line {problem.lineStart}` unconditionally** (`:207`). It is handed only
  the edited block's problems, so a line-less finding should never reach it — but that is a prop, not
  a type, and it is worth the thirty seconds in step 5 of *Validation* to see it.
- **`SettingsDialog.test.tsx` hard-codes its `titles` array** (`:67-85`); the failure names a count,
  not the missing title.
- **A `FORM` block is not unread.** It yields `Intent::Form`, and the orders inside it belong to a
  unit that does not exist yet. Treating it as unread would silence the check for every unit that
  forms anything.

## Context

Fifth slice of **ah-k6i**, the largest, on **ah-k6i.1** (planned; blocks this — `runReported`,
`failedStatus`, `warningStatus`). The path from a chosen file to a turn on screen lives in five
handlers of `AppShell.tsx` (`origin/main` `deb434a`, before .1): `applyReport` (:941-1036) — set
the report, commit it (`rememberTurn`, `gameMemory.ts:81`) unless a batch already did, read the
draft (`documentFor`, `orderDraft.ts:107`), build the status with counts, pick an opening hex if
nothing is selected; `storeReportOnly` (:1048-1084) — commit an older report for history without
touching the screen (gh-208); `loadReport` (:1086-1153) — flush, parse, `decideReportLoad`
(`reportLoadDecision.ts`), then ask / store-only / apply; `switchFaction` (:1156-1170) and
`mergeReport` (:1181-1220) — the two answers to the foreign-report prompt. Every decision that is
not already in `reportLoadDecision.ts` or `gameMemory.ts` sits inside the component: what a loaded
turn consists of, the prompt's snapshot of viewer and incoming, the older-turn message, the opening
selection. `restoreLatestTurn` (`gameMemory.ts:283-330`) already shows the shape: a plain function
that returns a `RestoredTurn` for the shell to apply in one place.

This bead moves the loading into `packages/shared/src/reportLoad.ts` — a `LoadedTurn` the shell
applies with **one** function — and collapses the five handlers to `runReported` calls. **One
player-visible change, chosen by the navigator (2026-08-15):** the report is shown only once its map
and orders came with it, so a commit or draft read that fails leaves the *previous* turn on screen
under the same red `could not read <file>: <error>` status — nothing half-applied (today the parsed
report is set first and stays up over an empty map when the second half fails). Everything else the
player sees is unchanged: strings, prompts, counts, busy.

