### The whole-turn test, which is where a mistake here would really show

`crates/core/tests/validate_real_orders.rs:91`
(`the_committed_turn_has_no_semantic_problems_either`) runs `check_turn` over turn 71's real report
and its own template — 477 units — and asserts **zero findings**; `:168` re-asserts it fifty times.
It is not in `semantics.rs` and is easy to miss.

**It stays green, and here is why, so a red there is recognised as a real defect rather than
accepted as expected churn.** That template holds exactly one `@sail`, no `BUY`, no `SELL`, no
`TAKE`, and 136 `@give 0 all <item>` discards. A discard is `Party::Discard`, which `party_id`
(`:495`) maps to `None`, so it charges the giver and credits nobody: every priced movement in that
turn makes a unit **lighter**. A load that only falls cannot cross a capacity it was already under,
so a silent fleet stays silent. The damaged-template case at `:128` injects
`GIVE 13401 999999999 SILV`, and `SILV` weighs 0.

If that test goes red, the most likely cause is a sign error in `moved` — crediting where the code
should charge — and the message will say `loaded this month` where it should say `unloaded`.

- Rust: `cargo test -p atlantis-hud-core`, which includes `validate_real_orders`.
- TypeScript: nothing to run beyond `pnpm run check:fast`'s own suites — no TS file changes.
- Smoke/PWA: not affected; no test enumerates the Warnings tab, and no code or toggle is added.

## User-facing decisions

Interviewed over three rounds with the navigator on 2026-08-17, against three mockups. The chosen
one is committed at **`docs/ui/fleet-load-after-loading.html`** (PR #346, merged), and carries all
five decisions and the states. Read it before starting; it shows the numbers in place, which the
prose below can only describe.

**1. The number is shown as a sum, not as one figure.** Chosen so the first number agrees with the
`Load:` line the report printed, which is on screen next to it.
*Rejected:* leaving `ah-j0e`'s sentence with only a better number in it — the report says
`Load: 110/750` and the warning would say 2610, with nothing explaining the gap; and
"2610 aboard after this month's gifts", which explains the gap without naming the size of the
mistake.

**2. What counts: everything the ledger prices — `GIVE`, `TAKE`, `BUY`, `SELL`.** Taken after the
correction in *Context*.
*Rejected:* the bead's own `GIVE`/`TAKE`-only scope, which was written on a false premise about the
turn order, stays silent when a ship is loaded from the market, keeps warning falsely when cargo is
sold to make room, and is more code.

**3. The verb is loaded / unloaded.** True of a gift and a purchase alike, and a symmetric pair.
*Rejected:* "taken aboard / taken ashore" — "taken" collides with the `TAKE` order, only one of
several things counted; "gained / given up" — vaguer, and "gained" reads ambiguously between the
fleet and the faction.

**4. An order nothing can price falls back to the report's weights; it does not silence the
fleet.** So this bead only ever adds warnings and never removes one that ships today.
*Rejected:* going silent for that fleet, the strict reading of the module's accept-on-doubt header.
The navigator's reason: a fleet visibly overloaded in the report saying nothing because of an
unrelated `WITHDRAW`, and a warning already on screen vanishing when the player adds one, are worse
than a number that may be stale. Recorded as knowingly the first uncertain line in the panel.

**5. A unit boarding this month counts as weight loaded, not as weight already aboard.** So the
first number is the report's own `Load:` figure in every case.
*Rejected:* summing the report weights of everyone aboard after `ENTER`/`LEAVE`, which keeps one
definition of the first number and leaves `ah-j0e`'s boarding message untouched, but loses the
agrees-with-the-report property that decision 1 was made for. Accepted cost: a fleet overloaded
purely by boarding changes from `310 aboard on a capacity of 150` to
`50 aboard plus 200 loaded this month, on a capacity of 150`.

**How decision 5 is implemented, and why it is the same thing.** The first number is computed as
the sum of the report weights of the units the report shows in the hull, **not** read off the
`Load:` line. On a real report the two are the same figure — the server computes its `Load:` the
same way, verified against `neworigins-3.0.0-g3-f42-t41.rep:2018` (states 110, holds 50 + 50 + 10)
and `neworigins-3.0.0-g5-f21-t24.rep:1359` (states 220, holds 20 + 200). Summing is chosen because
it also works for a fleet whose report states no `Load:` line, and because it cannot produce the
one thing worse than a stale number: if a report ever disagreed with its own units, reading the
stated figure would make the message claim weight moved this month when nothing did. If the two
ever do disagree, this degrades to the rejected option 5, which is merely a different true reading —
never to a false statement.

**The four sentences, verbatim. Assert them exactly; do not reword.**

```
Longship [329] is overloaded: 190 aboard on a capacity of 150, so it will not sail
Longship [329] is overloaded: 50 aboard plus 150 loaded this month, on a capacity of 150, so it will not sail
Longship [329] is overloaded: 300 aboard less 100 unloaded this month, on a capacity of 150, so it will not sail
Longship [329] is short of sailors: 2 sailing levels aboard, it needs 4, so it will not sail
```

The first is `ah-j0e`'s, unchanged, and is what most fleets get. The fourth is the crew half, not
touched by this bead. Plain integers, no thousands separators. `Longship [329]` comes from
`fleet_label`; several hulls read `Fleet [988] (8 Corsairs)`.

**Settings, panel and states, all confirmed unchanged:** the `fleet-overloaded` code, the
`Overloaded fleets` toggle under `Sailing`, the line the finding points at (the captain's `SAIL`),
exactly-at-capacity sailing in silence, silence for a foreign unit in the hull, silence when a
`MOVE` touches the fleet, silence with no report, and load-silent-crew-still-judged when a unit's
weight is unstated.

## Out of scope

- **The crew half of the check.** Sailing levels are read from each unit's report skills and are not
  moved by a `GIVE` of men, so giving your sailors away still shows the old crew number. Related but
  separate: `ah-paj`, filed while planning this, is the defect that those levels are counted once
  per unit rather than once per man.
- **`BUY ALL ITEM`.** `buy` (`semantics.rs:558-561`) returns without crediting anything, because how
  many it buys depends on the purse and the market's stock. So it adds no weight. A missed warning,
  never a false one; leave it.
- **`WITHDRAW`, `GIVE x UNIT`, `TAKE ALL` from a unit outside the hex.** Unpriceable by the ledger,
  so they contribute nothing and the report's weights stand — decision 4, not a gap to close here.
- **`ah-j0e`'s own scope**: which fleets are checked, who the captain is, the `ENTER`/`LEAVE`
  membership rule, capacity resolution, the doubt cases that silence a whole fleet. All unchanged.
- **Reading the `Load:` line's first number.** Deliberately not done; see decision 5's
  implementation note. Do not add a `stated_cargo_load` to `mode.rs`.
- **"Within a man of not sailing"**, fleet speed, and whether the route itself is sailable.

