## Known traps

- **Every existing sailing test passes unchanged, and that is a check on the work rather than a
  coincidence.** If one goes red, the implementation is wrong — do not edit the test. Why each
  survives, with `reported` = sum of report weights of units the report shows in the hull:
  `a_fleet_over_capacity_...` has `reported` 190 and load 190, so `change` is 0 and the plain
  sentence with `190 aboard` is emitted, exactly as asserted; `stated_numbers_beat_the_ruleset` has
  `reported` 5401 and load 5401, so `capacity of 5400` still appears; `both_problems_...`,
  `a_bare_sail_is_a_sail` and `a_unit_entering_this_month_counts_aboard` assert codes only; every
  silent test has no transfers, so no weight moves. The one edit is the added message assertion on
  `a_unit_entering_this_month_counts_aboard`.
- **`clippy::comparison_chain` is on by default and `-D warnings` makes it fatal.** Writing the
  three message forms as `if change == 0 {} else if change > 0 {} else {}` fails the gate. The
  `match` on `Option` with guards given above does not, and neither would `match change.cmp(&0)`.
- **`rustc` warns on an unused function parameter**, and `check:fast`'s
  `cargo clippy -- -D warnings` turns that into a failure. This is why increment 1 stops short of
  giving `check_sailing` its ledger.
- **A hull the report shows empty gives `reported == Some(0)`, not `None`** — `Option`'s `Sum` over
  an empty iterator is `Some(0)`. A fleet everybody boards this month therefore reads
  `0 aboard plus 250 loaded this month, on a capacity of 150`, which is correct and agrees with a
  report stating `Load: 0/150`. Do not "fix" it into the plain form.
- **A test whose giver does not hold what it gives gets two findings**, because `check_resources`
  emits `not-enough-items` from the same ledger, and `only(...)` then panics on the count. Stock
  every giver.
- **A captain without `sail(4)` gets `fleet-undercrewed` as a second finding**, for the same reason.
  `longship("329")` states `Sailors: 4/4`.
- **`Ordered::holding` is the report's amount, `Ledger::balance` is the amount after orders.** The
  difference between them is the whole of what this bead adds. Do not read `unit.items` as if it
  were the balance, and do not read the balance as if it were the holding.
- **Silver weighs 0**, so a test built on a silver gift proves nothing about weight — and neither
  does one whose fleet is silent both before and after. Use grain (`GRAI`, 5), horses (`HORS`, 50)
  or gnolls (`GNOL`, 10, a man). **There is no `VIKI` in this catalogue**, whatever Atlantis
  elsewhere calls its vikings; the fourteen man tags are `LEAD WELF HELF IDWA HDWA UDWA ORC GNOM
  CTAU HUMN LIZA GBLN GNOL DRLF`. A tag the ruleset does not carry is silently weighed at nothing,
  so a test built on one passes for the wrong reason.
- **`ledger_for` must apply intents unit-major, exactly as `check_resources` does today** — outer
  loop over `hex.units`, inner over that unit's intents. That is not document order across units,
  and it changes what `GIVE ALL` resolves to. It is pre-existing behaviour that the shortfall
  warnings already depend on; inheriting it is correct, and "fixing" it here would change findings
  this bead has nothing to do with.
- **The ledger applies orders in document order, which is not the server's phase order.** A unit
  that writes `BUY 10 GRAI` and then `GIVE 8801 ALL GRAI` gives away grain the server would not have
  bought yet. Rare, bounded, and pre-existing in the shortfall check; do not engineer around it.
- **The bead's description is wrong about the turn order.** If it and this plan disagree, the plan
  is right and the citation is in *Context*. The bead text was not rewritten.
- **`ah-paj` will show up during the look-once step** as a spurious `Undercrewed fleets` line on the
  Raft. It is a separate, already-filed bug. Do not fix it here, and do not let it fail the manual
  check.

## Context

A faction may only conduct trade activity — which the rules define to include producing — in so
many regions a month. Order production in one region too many and the engine refuses that whole
region's orders: `PRODUCE: Faction can't produce in that many regions.`, five times across three
turns of the committed corpus.

**The arithmetic was checked by hand and works exactly.** In
`tests/fixtures/reports/neworigins-3.0.0-g3-f42-t41.rep`:

```
:52  Faction Status:
:53  Tax Regions: 14 (15)
:54  Trade Regions: 15 (15)
…
:62    Smiths (12256): PRODUCE: Faction can't produce in that many regions.
:63    Miners (11127): PRODUCE: Faction can't produce in that many regions.
```

Its orders template (from `:2058`) carries 40 depth-0 `PRODUCE` orders across **16 distinct
regions**. 16 − 15 = one region refused; that region is `tundra (35,9) in Bedba`; and it holds
exactly the two units the report errors on. Confirmed from the other end too — the report's own
`Produces N x in <region>` events name 15 regions, and the one template region missing from them is
`tundra (35,9)`.

**Every `PRODUCE` counts, whatever it makes.** The rules say trade activity "includes producing
goods and materials" (`tests/fixtures/ruleset/neworigins-rules.html:623-629`) and draw no
item-type distinction. The refused region proves it: unit 11127 produced `IRON` straight from the
land (a *primary* produce) and 12256 produced `swor` from iron (a *manufacturing* produce), and both
were refused. This matters because it means **the item argument is never needed** and
`Intent::MonthLong("PRODUCE")` as it stands is enough — no parser change, no new intent.

("Trade goods" in the rules are something else entirely: `:3055` says "All items except silver and
trade goods are produced with the PRODUCE order", so they are the items that cannot be produced at
all. Do not go looking for a trade-goods flag in the ruleset; there is none, and none is wanted.)

**Two report schemas, both live in the corpus, and the check must read both:**

| schema | fixtures | meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `Tax Regions: 14 (15)` and `Trade Regions: 15 (15)` | `g2-f42`, all `g3-f42` | two counters, two separate pools |
| `Regions: 10 (10)` | `g4`, all `g5`, `g7`, `g8` | one counter pooling taxing **and** trading |

`report/header.rs:60-63` already anticipates this: *"Key-agnostic: NewOrigins prints `Regions`,
standard Atlantis prints `Tax Regions` and `Trade Regions`. The label is whatever the ruleset
printed."* Under the pooled schema a `TAX` order costs a region the same as a `PRODUCE` does, and
that checks out on `g5-f21-t25.rep`: 9 produce regions ∪ 2 tax-only regions = 11 against
`Regions: 10 (10)`, and the eleventh (`swamp (35,47) in Pangmore`) is refused with both its
producers erroring.

**This is the first finding in the module that belongs to the faction rather than to a hex**, and
that is not a free choice: `Finding.region_id` is a plain `String` (`semantics.rs:135`, doc: *"Every
finding has one; that is what makes them filterable by hex"*), and the client drops a hexless
diagnostic on purpose (`packages/shared/src/orderEditor.ts:194`, `if (diagnostic.regionId === null)
continue;`). A finding with no hex would vanish from the panel **and** from the header count. The
navigator's answer to that is decision 1 below, and it keeps the whole bead in Rust.

## Files to change, and what to reuse

Line numbers are against `origin/main` at `5a33e77`. Everything is in
`crates/core/src/orders/semantics.rs` except the settings entry.

### The code

```rust
    pub const TOO_MANY_TRADE_REGIONS: Code = Code("too-many-trade-regions");
```

appended to `codes::ALL` (`:84`), which is `[Code; 12]` today. **Count what is there when you
arrive**: `ah-djq`, `ah-yfo` and `ah-d8u` each add one to the same array and to the same *Orders*
group, and any of them may land first.

### What the faction may afford

```rust
/// What this faction may spend on trade this month, and whether taxing draws on the same pool.
///
/// Two schemas are in the corpus and both are live. Older reports print `Tax Regions: 14 (15)` and
/// `Trade Regions: 15 (15)`, two counters over two pools. Newer ones print one `Regions: 10 (10)`
/// pooling both, and there a TAX order costs a region exactly as a PRODUCE does. The label is
/// whatever the ruleset printed (`report/header.rs:60-63`), so the specific one is asked for first
/// and the pooled one second; a report with neither is left alone rather than guessed at.
///
/// The `used` figure is deliberately ignored. It counts what *last* month spent, and the question
/// here is what this month's orders would spend.
fn trade_allowance(report: &ParsedReport) -> Option<(i64, bool)> {
    let entries = &report.header.faction_status.entries;
    let labelled = |label: &str| {
        entries
            .iter()
            .find(|entry| entry.label.eq_ignore_ascii_case(label))
    };

    if let Some(entry) = labelled("Trade Regions") {
        return Some((entry.maximum, false));
    }
    labelled("Regions").map(|entry| (entry.maximum, true))
}
```

`report.header.faction_status.entries` is `Vec<FactionStatusEntry { label, used, maximum }>`
(`report/header.rs:73-81`), reachable from `check_turn`'s `report: &ParsedReport` with no plumbing
(`report/mod.rs:43-44`, `header.rs:56`).

### Counting what the document orders

`check_turn` already builds one `Hex` per region. Collect from that loop rather than walking the
report twice. Above the loop:

