## User-facing decisions

One question, asked 2026-08-16 (Beast → navigator): the message shown when a backup file is
refused for a shape error (a missing/mistyped field), surfaced by the shell as
`could not import <file>: <message>`. Options: (A) one line plus serde's field detail —
`backup file could not be read: missing field \`importedTurns\` at line 4 column 1`; (B) keep the
web's ~40 per-field messages ("backup file imported turn 3 is missing its faction id"), written
once in Rust as a hand validator. **Chosen: A.** B rejected as ~150 lines that must track every
new field for a message a player sees only with a corrupt file.

Everything else keeps its existing wording, exactly: `backup file is not valid JSON`,
`backup file is not an Atlantis HUD game export`, `backup file does not say which version it is`,
`backup file format version {n} is newer than this build supports ({max})`,
`backup file format version {n} is not supported`, `invalid game manifest version: expected <= 1,
got {n}`, `invalid game backup: remembered region {id} …` (list under `BackupError`). No mockup —
no layout changes.

## Out of scope

- The rules on what a *report import* writes (supersede, `importedAt` on re-import) — ah-8m0.2.
- Latest-turn ranking and list ordering (`load_latest_imported_turn`, `list_hex_notes`,
  `listImportedTurns`) — ah-8m0.3. The ORDER BYs of those reads are untouched here; only the five
  in `export_game` go.
- Generating TS types from Rust (ah-164) or the command boundary (ah-wxk). `DecodedGameBackup` is
  hand-declared like `PreparedImport` beside it.
- Bumping `CURRENT_GAME_BACKUP_VERSION`: the file format does not change — same fields, same
  names; only the writer/reader moves. A v1 file written before this bead imports after it.
- `packages/shared/src/gameBackup.ts` (the picker's peek) and `backupAsCopy`.
- Making the web import transactional across its five stores (it stays five awaits + rollback).

## Validation

```bash
cargo test -p atlantis-hud-core backup            # the codec's tests
cargo test --workspace
cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings && cargo fmt --check
node scripts/ensure-wasm.mjs && pnpm --filter @atlantis/browser-core test
git grep -n 'parseBackupJson\|GAME_BACKUP_FORMAT\|CURRENT_GAME_BACKUP_VERSION' packages crates -- ':!packages/browser-core/src/wasm'
#   → only crates/core/src/backup.rs (the two constants) and nothing in packages/
git grep -n 'GameBackup' crates/core-persistence/src/lib.rs   # only `atlantis_hud_core::backup::` paths and the tests' literals
pnpm run check:fast && pnpm run check
```

Human check: none required. Psylocke may export a game on the desktop and import the file on
the web (and the reverse) — both directions must round-trip; that is the whole point.

## Known traps

- **Judge the envelope before the body, or the smoke test breaks.** `tests/smoke/backup.spec.ts:224`
  feeds a `version: 99` backup with no `exportedAt`; if `decode` deserialises the whole struct first
  it reports a missing field instead of "newer than this build supports". Two stages, as specified.
- **`#[serde(flatten)]` and `to_js`.** `DecodedRegionSighting` flattens `RegionSighting`; core-wasm's
  `to_js` (:19–29) already turns map-shaped output into plain objects, so the fields land at the
  top level as `regionId`, `lastSeenTurn`, `payloadJson`, `x`, … Use `to_js`, never
  `serde_wasm_bindgen::to_value` directly.
- **wasm-bindgen throws strings.** `Err(JsValue::from_str(..))` reaches JS as a thrown string, not
  an `Error`. The `importGame` wrapper above turns it into an `Error`; without it
  `rejects.toThrow(/…/u)` in the new tests fails even though the shell would have displayed it.
- **The wasm module is gitignored and stale until rebuilt.** After touching `crates/`, run
  `node scripts/ensure-wasm.mjs` (or `pnpm --filter @atlantis/browser-core typecheck`, whose
  `pretypecheck` runs it) before `gameBackup.wasm.test.ts` — the test loads
  `src/wasm/atlantis_core.js` from disk and would call functions that are not there yet.
- **`readReport("g7f95t71")` for the real module.** `webCoreAdapter.test.ts`'s `REPORT` literal
  is two lines that only the fake parses; the real core refuses it. Use the fixture, as
  `parseReportFull.test.ts` does.
- **Do not `pub use` more than the three manifest types from `core-persistence`.** core-tauri
  imports `GameManifest, GameMetadata, ReportSourceRef` from this crate (`core-tauri/src/lib.rs:16-18`);
  the `GameBackup*` names had no importer outside the crate — check with
  `git grep -n 'GameBackup' crates/core-tauri apps` (nothing) before deciding not to re-export them.
- **`ensure_supported_manifest_version` stays in core-persistence** (used by `create_game`,
  `open_game`, `export_game`); only its constant moves.
- **Sorting the export changes nothing a player sees but changes bytes.** No existing test
  compares an export string literally on either side (checked); the new
  `encode_writes_the_envelope_and_sorts_every_table` is the first, so quote its expected literal
  from the actual output once the shape is right, then keep it.
- **The smoke suites take the machine-wide gate lock** (`scripts/withGateLock.ts`); run
  `pnpm run check` once at the end, not per increment.
